3.60.m:
at Gaṅgādvāra, Kuśāvarta, Bilvaka, Nīlaparvata, / likewise at the Kanakhala pilgrimage place, and likewise Śāligrāma;
at Vārāṇasī, at the Sarasvatī, at the confluence of the Gaṅgā and the ocean, / at the meeting of the Sindhu and the ocean, and at the Tritakūpa, O sinless ones;
at the holy Plakṣaprasravaṇa, and at Camasonmajjana; / at Mahālaya, Kedāra, and likewise Bhṛgutuṅga;
at the holy Mānasa, and likewise Uttaramānasa; / in these and other pilgrimage places with sacred waters that may exist,
and on the banks of rivers, it should be known to bear abundant fruit. / Likewise, what is given to the poor, the blind, the wretched, the helpless, the mute,
the disabled and others—the dull-witted, the dwarf, the lame— / and to those afflicted with disease, that wealth yields abundant fruit.
What is given as aid for dharma, when a sacrifice is being performed, / or for solemnizing a marriage, is proclaimed imperishable.
A man through whose wealth received someone enters into festive joy / becomes a sharer in festivals wherever he is born.

3.62.m:
