he attains great beauty and is also freed from diseases.
Thus the gift of cows and the products of cows yields great fruit.

As many hairs as there are on the cow, so much reward the giver of the cow obtains;
and he rescues his sons, grandsons, and entire family up to the seventh generation in the other world.
Haṃsa said:
Understand, O best of the twice-born, the gift of a ghee-cow:
one should prepare a pot filled with clarified butter of cow-milk;
and likewise one should prepare a full water jar as her calf,
covered with a bronze lid, accompanied by white cloth,
both containing gold within and smeared on the outside;
her legs made of sugarcane stalks, and the hooves made of silver;
the two eyes should be made of gold, and the two horns made of aloe-wood;
and seven grains should be prepared placed separately on her sides;
her cloth should be of silk, and her blanket [also], O best of the twice-born;
one should place frankincense far on her breath, and her udder made of gold;
likewise the tongue made of sugar, the face made of molasses and milk,
the tail of flax-thread, and the hairs of white mustard seeds;

3.64.m:
likewise of spiced rice under Ārdrā, and of silver under [the asterism of] Aditi; / of clarified butter under Puṣya, and of perfumes under the serpent-asterism;
of sesame seeds under the conjunction of Maghā, of panic seed under the deity Bhaga; / of sweet cakes under the asterism of Aryaman, and of milk-rice under that of Savitṛ;
of multicoloured garments under Citrā, of roasted grain flour under the deity Vāyu; / of metals under [the asterism of] Indra and Agni, and of a garland and fruit under that of Mitra;
likewise of an umbrella under Śāka, and of roots and fruits under Mūla; / the gift of snow with honey under the asterism of the Waters yields great fruit;
of food under [the asterism of] the Viśvedevas, and of a garment under Śravaṇa; / of grain under [that of] the Vasus, O brahmins, and of medicine under that of Varuṇa;
of seeds under [the asterism of] Aja Ekapād, and immediately after, of crops; / likewise of cow's milk products under that of Pūṣan, and of baths under that of the Aśvins;
and the gift of sesame seeds under the Bharaṇīs bears great fruit. / Next, on the first lunar day, of flowers, and on the second, of clarified butter;

on the third, of garments, and on the fourth, of gold; / on the fifth, of fruits, and on the sixth, of a bath, O honour-givers;
and on the seventh, of sweet cakes, and on the eighth, of molasses; / on the ninth, of gruel, and on the tenth, of silver;
on the eleventh, of gold, and on the twelfth, of clothing; / on the thirteenth, of fragrant substances, and immediately after, of white sugar;

He should please the horse-headed deity [Hayaśiras] by giving a horse in Āśvina; / he attains the world of Viṣṇu and elevates his lineage.
In Kārttika, by giving a woolen garment, he attains heaven. / The gift of salt in Mārgaśīrṣa brings great reward.
Likewise [the gift] of grains in Pauṣa, and of firewood in the following [month, Māgha]; / and of all perfumes in Phālguna; here no doubt should be entertained.
The Caitra twelfth conjoined with the asterism Bhāgya (Pūrvaphālgunī) brings great reward; / the Vaiśākhī joined with Hasta; likewise in Jyeṣṭha with Svātī;
and in Āṣāḍha with Jyeṣṭhā; and in the month of Śrāvaṇa with Mūla; / likewise in the month of Bhādrapada joined with Śravaṇa;
in Āśvina the holy twelfth day is conjoined with the Aja asterism (Pūrvabhādrapadā); / in Kārttika joined with Revatī; and in the month of Mārgaśīrṣa with Kṛttikā;
in Pauṣa conjoined with Mṛgaśiras; in Māgha joined with the asterism of Āditya (Punarvasu); / and in Phālguna conjoined with Puṣya: such a twelfth day is supremely purifying.
When fasting on these [twelfth days] conjoined with these asterisms, the gift [then given], / O best of the twice-born, is known to yield the fulfillment of all desires and to be endless.

At the transit of the sun into Aries, the gift of a ram brings great reward. / At the transit into Taurus, the gift of cattle is likewise proclaimed;
and the gift of a bed and a seat when the sun enters Gemini. / At the entrance into Cancer, [the gift] of roasted barley flour and sugar candy,
and of molasses, treacle, candy, and white sugar, O twice-born, / and of salt, clarified butter, and fruit, as well as green herbs;
and likewise the gift of water jars is known to yield great reward. / At the entrance into Leo, [the gift] of vessels made of metal;
at the entrance into Virgo, of fragrant garments; / at the entrance into Libra, [the gift] of grains and seeds is excellent;
at the entrance into Scorpio, the gift of garments and dwellings; / at the entrance into Sagittarius, [the gift] of garments and vehicles yields great reward;
at the entrance into Capricorn, the gift of firewood and fire; / at the entrance into Aquarius, the gift of grass for cattle; / at the entrance into Pisces, [the gift] of dwelling places and garlands is best.
